module gunbc.scm.commit_closure

// WHAT A COMMIT CLOSURE IS, INDEPENDENT OF HOW IT IS WRITTEN DOWN.
//
// A closure is a set of content-addressed objects plus the root the commit names. That is the whole
// logical content. Positions, JSON, member names and format tags are all facts about ONE WAY of
// writing it down, and none of them belong here -- this module is what every realization must agree
// about, and a realization is chosen beside it, never inside it.
//
// THE PROPERTY THIS MODULE EXISTS TO MAKE REPRESENTABLE, and it is the reason the format it replaces
// had to be recut rather than extended:
//
//   A closure may be INCOMPLETE, and an incomplete closure must be able to NAME WHAT IS MISSING.
//
// The predecessor format could not express that, and not by oversight. It recorded structure only --
// kind, edge labels, and which EARLIER POSITION each edge pointed at -- with every identity re-derived
// on load. A backward position cannot name an object the document does not contain, so an unresolved
// reference named nothing at all and "supply the missing object" was unperformable. The disposition
// was absent BY CONSTRUCTION, not by low population.
//
// WHY THAT FORMAT WAS RIGHT TO DO IT, because the recut gives something up and must say so. Omitting
// identities was a construction wall, not an accident: a decoder is a SECOND PRODUCER, reading
// identities from a file it does not control, and a wire that carries an identity is a wire a decoder
// can lie in. With no such field, forging an identity is not caught -- it is UNWRITABLE. The store
// would otherwise answer for a locator whose payload was never hashed to it, and checkout would
// reconstruct a program the commit never named.
//
// SO THE TWO PROPERTIES ARE ONE DECISION SEEN FROM TWO SIDES:
//
//   unforgeable identity   =>  no identity on the wire
//   nameable absent object =>  an identity on the wire
//
// The recut keeps both by asking what each reference NEEDS to name. A reference to an object the
// document CONTAINS needs no identity -- the object is right there, and its identity is derived from
// its own bytes. Only a reference to an object the document LACKS needs one, and that is the single
// place a digest cannot do the damage the predecessor feared: there is NO PAYLOAD IN THE DOCUMENT for
// a forged digest to be mis-bound to. So the forgeable surface is exactly the set of objects the
// document does not contain -- the set for which forgery has nothing to bind.
//
// THE TRUST ROOT MOVES, AND THIS IS THE HONEST STATEMENT OF WHAT IS WEAKER.
//
// Deriving a supplied object's identity from its own bytes and comparing proves "I got the object THIS
// DOCUMENT NAMED". It does NOT prove "I got the object the COMMIT named" -- a forged uncontained-arm digest
// names an attacker-chosen object, and the supplied impostor matches the forgery exactly because that
// is what was forged. The closure then reconstructs with the impostor in place of the real object: a
// program the commit never named, reached through the very arm this recut adds.
//
// That is closed by ONE CONDITION, stated here as a condition rather than assumed -- and the
// condition is about the LOGICAL ROOT IDENTITY, not about the bytes:
//
//   The root identity DERIVED from contained structure plus uncontained child identities MUST be
//   compared against an expected commit identity the consumer obtains from OUTSIDE the document.
//
// This needs no new machinery, because object_store already derives a record's identity from its
// kind and its children's target identities. An uncontained child contributes its stated digest to
// its parent's derived identity and transitively to the root's, so substituting Hc with Hx changes
// the derived root. A forged document is therefore a document that does not reconstruct to the
// commit you asked for, and the comparison catches it.
//
// WHY NOT HASH THE WIRE BYTES, which was this clause's first form and was wrong. Byte identity makes
// representation detail semantic: the same logical closure emitted with a different object-table
// order, or under a different encoding entirely, would become a DIFFERENT commit. That undoes the
// separation this whole recut exists to make -- the logical closure is the concept, a serialization
// is one way of writing it down. Two identities, kept apart:
//
//   commit identity      = the derived Merkle root over canonical logical structure
//   publication identity = an optional digest of exact publication bytes, for corruption detection
//                          or envelope authentication, and NEVER the semantic commit identity
//
// But note precisely what changed: the predecessor's guarantee was UNCONDITIONAL -- the format had no
// field to lie in -- and this one is CONDITIONAL on an expected commit identity being obtained
// independently. A reader of this format must not inherit the old file's unconditional claim. It is
// strictly weaker, it is the price of being able to name what is missing, and it is paid knowingly.
//
// AND THE RUNG OF THE DIGEST ITSELF IS NOT AUTHENTICATION. ObjectId is fnv1a64 structural. It
// establishes structural consistency and refuses collisions at the current local rung; it is not an
// adversarial or cross-tenant cryptographic commitment, and calling this "Merkle" must not import
// that stronger claim. dissolve-on: a computing cryptographic digest reachable from .dag.

import std.types { Bool, Int, List, list_length }
import gunbc.scm.object_store {
  ObjectId,
  ObjectRecord,
  ObjectStore,
  StoredEdge,
  object_id_eq,
  store_contains,
  store_records_in_dependency_order,
}

// WHY AN ADMISSION IS A VALUE AND NOT A FLAG, and this is the difference between moving a wall and
// keeping one.
//
// Before the recut, an incomplete closure COULD NOT BE WRITTEN: the encoder computed which references
// were unresolvable and refused. After the recut it CAN be written -- that is the entire point -- and
// the danger is that the only thing left stopping an accidental partial write is a caller who
// remembers to check. That is validation, and validation of exactly the shape that decays: the
// encoder accepts a closure plus a caller's assurance, and one day the assurance is wrong.
//
// So the uncontained arm is constructible ONLY from an evidenced decision. The arm CARRIES the admission,
// the admission is `sole_constructor` and minted here, and an encoder therefore cannot fabricate one
// to get itself out of trouble -- it has to be HANDED one by whoever decided a partial closure was
// publishable. An undeclared partial closure has no constructor, so it is not refused, it is
// unwritable, which is where the predecessor's guarantee lived too.
//
// THE REVIEW TELL, stated so it can be checked against a later diff: if a consumer of this module
// grows a check that the encoder used to perform, the wall was MOVED rather than kept, and this
// paragraph is the thing that was violated.
// THE RUNG THIS ACTUALLY OCCUPIES, AND IT IS LOWER THAN TWO REVIEWERS READ IT AS.
//
// `sole_constructor` stops a caller writing the RECORD LITERAL. It does not stop a caller calling
// the public mint below, because .dag has no module privacy -- so the token is unforgeable in shape
// and freely OBTAINABLE in practice. Worse, it is not bound to any subject:
//
//   admit closure A  ->  token T
//   encode closure B with T  ->  ACCEPTED
//
// THE ADMISSION IS BOUND TO ITS SUBJECT, and that binding is the whole guarantee.
//
// THE DEFECT THIS REPLACES. The predecessor was PartialClosureAdmission { reason } -- a token minted
// by a public function, carrying no subject. The encoder took a closure PLUS an optional token and
// checked only that a token was PRESENT, never that it was ABOUT the closure being encoded:
//
//     admit closure A -> token T
//     encode closure B with T -> ACCEPTED
//
// That is authority substitution in its exact form: a true admission about one subject answering for
// another because no relation binds them. sole_constructor did not help, because `.dag` has no
// module privacy -- it blocks the record literal while the public mint stays freely callable. And
// the mutation that existed (delete the check) could never have caught it: deleting a check proves
// the check is READ, which a bearer token satisfies perfectly.
//
// WHY THE REPAIR IS A SHAPE, NOT A CHECK. The carrier holds the closure it admits, so the encoder
// takes ONE argument and there is no second closure to disagree with it. "A token for A used on B"
// is not refused at runtime -- it has no spelling. The mismatch is unconstructible rather than
// caught, which is DESIGN section 4b's top rung and the reason no validator appears beside this.
//
// `unresolved` is DERIVED here from the closure rather than supplied, so it cannot disagree with the
// objects it describes; a caller-supplied population would reintroduce the same substitution one
// field down. The mint refuses a COMPLETE closure, because admitting a partial write for something
// with nothing missing is a category error and would make the carrier's own name a lie.
//
// REMAINING RUNG, stated so it is not read as more than it is: `unresolved` is a List, so an EMPTY
// admitted population is representable in the type even though this mint cannot produce one. The
// corpus has no NonEmptyList, and minting one solely for this field would grow net concepts to buy a
// guarantee the mint's refusal already provides -- so the emptiness exclusion sits at mitigatable
// while the SUBJECT BINDING, which is what the defect was about, is structural. Next-rung trigger: a
// NonEmptyList authority earning its place from more than one consumer.
type AdmittedPartialCommitClosure sole_constructor {
  closure: CommitClosure
  unresolved: List<ObjectId>
  reason: PartialClosureReason
}

// THE REFUSAL IS AN ARM OF THE OUTCOME, not a one-member coproduct beside it. There is exactly one
// way to be refused here -- the closure has nothing missing -- and wrapping that in its own type
// would either read as a type alias or invite a second arm invented to justify the wrapper. If a
// second genuine refusal appears, it joins this coproduct and every match fails to compile HERE,
// which is the behaviour the nesting was for.
type PartialClosureAdmissionOutcome
  = PartialClosureAdmitted { admitted: AdmittedPartialCommitClosure }
  | ClosureCompleteSoNothingToAdmit

// WHY A CLOSED VOCABULARY RATHER THAN A STRING. A reason nobody can enumerate is a reason nobody can
// refuse, and the two admitted causes have genuinely different owners: one is a transfer decision,
// the other is a graft that object_store deliberately supports.
type PartialClosureReason
  = ClosureTrimmedForTransfer
  | ClosureGraftedWithoutChildren

fn admit_partial_commit_closure(
  closure: CommitClosure,
  reason: PartialClosureReason
) -> PartialClosureAdmissionOutcome {
  let unresolved = unresolved_identities(closure: closure)
  if list_length(items: unresolved) == 0 {
    ClosureCompleteSoNothingToAdmit
  } else {
    PartialClosureAdmitted {
      admitted: AdmittedPartialCommitClosure {
        closure: closure,
        unresolved: unresolved,
        reason: reason,
      }
    }
  }
}

fn admitted_reason(admitted: AdmittedPartialCommitClosure) -> PartialClosureReason {
  admitted.reason
}

fn admitted_closure(admitted: AdmittedPartialCommitClosure) -> CommitClosure {
  admitted.closure
}

fn admitted_unresolved(admitted: AdmittedPartialCommitClosure) -> List<ObjectId> {
  admitted.unresolved
}

// A closure is the store plus the root the commit names. Nothing about how it is written down.
type CommitClosure {
  store: ObjectStore
  root: ObjectId
}

// WHY THE WIRE ARM IS "UNCONTAINED" AND THIS FUNCTION SAYS "UNRESOLVED", which is not two names for
// one thing (DESIGN section 3) but two different facts one join apart.
//
// CONTAINED / UNCONTAINED is INTRINSIC to the document: it states whether this document carries the
// object's payload, and that is true of the bytes forever, wherever they are read. UNRESOLVED is
// RELATIVE: it is what you get by joining an uncontained identity against the store you actually
// have. An object this document does not carry may already sit in the local store, in another
// generation, in a cache, or at a remote -- so it is uncontained here and not unresolved for you.
//
// Naming the wire arm "absent" would have frozen a relative availability observation into immutable
// vocabulary, which is exactly the state-space conflation that makes a reader draw "missing" from a
// document that only ever said "not carried here".
//
//   uncontained identities  +  the store you have  ->  unresolved identities
//
// THE ACCEPTANCE TEST OF THE WHOLE RECUT, in one function.
//
// This is what the predecessor could not have at any price: the set of object identities the closure
// REFERS TO and DOES NOT CONTAIN. Each is a content address, so each names exactly what to fetch.
// A backward position could never appear in this list, because a position that does not resolve
// inside the document denotes nothing outside it.
//
// An EMPTY list is the complete closure. That is the ordinary case and it is not a special arm --
// completeness is "nothing is missing", derived, never a stored flag that could disagree with the
// objects beside it.
fn unresolved_identities(closure: CommitClosure) -> List<ObjectId> {
  concat(uncontained_targets(store: closure.store), unresolved_root(closure: closure))
}

// TABLE COMPLETENESS WITHOUT A ROOT, because two different consumers ask two different questions and
// only one of them has a root to ask about.
//
// A COMMIT closure asks "is anything this program reaches missing", which includes its own root. A
// REPOSITORY object table asks only "does any edge point at a record this table does not hold" --
// it carries many commits over one population, so there is no single root and the roots it does
// have are checked separately against the commit list. Folding the root into this would have forced
// the repository to invent one.
// UNCONTAINED TARGETS ACCUMULATE BY PREPEND AND ARE REVERSED ONCE, the same idiom this stack applies
// in commit_closure_json_v2 encode_step and in repository_envelope's commit fold. It is restated at
// the third site because the first two were not enough: this module documented the rule and then
// reintroduced the shape it forbids, which is the §6 point exactly -- fixing a cost shape where a
// review names it is not the same as not having the defect, and the sweep has to cover every
// accumulator a change introduces.
//
// BOTH LEVELS WERE QUADRATIC, and the inner one is why the per-record helper takes the caller's
// accumulator instead of returning a fresh list. Building a list per record and joining it at the
// top copies the whole accumulator once per RECORD; prepending each child onto the single threaded
// accumulator copies nothing at either level, and one linear reverse at the top restores authored
// order exactly.
//
// THE REVERSE IS LOAD-BEARING, AND THAT SENTENCE COST A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN AN ASSERTION. An
// earlier revision of this comment claimed order was already observed and named two existing claims
// as the evidence. It was false: names_exactly is a MEMBERSHIP fold and every closure fixture
// asserted exactly ONE missing identity, and a one-element list reversed is itself. Dropping the
// reverse was measured to leave every claim in the file green. The claim that holds it up is
// two_uncontained_children_are_named_in_order, written for this and verified both ways -- with the
// reverse exit=0, without it exit=1.
//
// The realized n is not the argument. A delta encoder folds this over the whole store, and DESIGN §6
// rejects "n is small here" as a non-time-stable fact: reuse is what changes n.
fn uncontained_targets(store: ObjectStore) -> List<ObjectId> {
  reverse(
    fold(
      store_records_in_dependency_order(store: store),
      init: [],
      f: fn(acc, record) { uncontained_in_record(store: store, acc: acc, record: record) }
    )
  )
}

fn uncontained_in_record(store: ObjectStore, acc: List<ObjectId>, record: ObjectRecord) -> List<ObjectId> {
  fold(
    record.children,
    init: acc,
    f: fn(inner, child) {
      if store_contains(store: store, identity: child.target) { inner } else { concat([child.target], inner) }
    }
  )
}

// THE ROOT IS A REFERENCE LIKE ANY OTHER, and a closure missing its own root is the sharpest case of
// incompleteness there is -- a document that names a commit whose top object it does not carry.
// The predecessor encoded the root positionally too, so this case was as unnameable as the rest.
fn unresolved_root(closure: CommitClosure) -> List<ObjectId> {
  if store_contains(store: closure.store, identity: closure.root) { [] } else { [closure.root] }
}

fn closure_is_complete(closure: CommitClosure) -> Bool {
  list_length(items: unresolved_identities(closure: closure)) == 0
}
